family: pure_mom
universe: risk
cutoff: 2026-07-23
min_new_rows: 126
rule: highest risk-adjusted score = mean OOS delta / max(mean OOS maxDD_pp, 5.0) across the v12 risk-budget bench's 9 test windows (BUILD_SPEC_V12.md 3)
resolved_from: records/experiments/alloc12_risk — pure_mom (full-size top-1 momentum over the RISK sleeve) was the frontier at risk-adjusted score +2.895 (+105.46 pp/yr mean OOS delta, 36.4% mean OOS maxDD, BEATS-SPX 6/9). The two NEW risk-limited families LOST the risk-adjusted score they were built for: rp_topk +0.801 (+25.06 pp/yr, 31.3% DD, 4/9 NO-EDGE) and vt_mom +0.760 (+23.45 pp/yr, 30.8% DD, 5/9). Sizing to a risk budget removed far more return than drawdown (return -78%, DD only -15%) because the momentum edge lives in the high-vol crypto winners that vol targeting caps. best_bh NO-EDGE score -0.240. The frontier metric was changed to risk-adjusted for the operator's "limit risk" mandate BEFORE the run, and it still names the full-size control — the risk-limited families lose on return-per-unit-drawdown too.
confirmatory: this forward re-arms pure_mom on the SAME crypto-era calendar/family as data/holdout/alloc11.FORWARD (spec v12 4 pre-declared the pure_mom-wins branch); it is confirmatory of that live forward, not a new mechanism test. v12's genuine finding is the negative one — risk-budgeting by position sizing does not improve risk-adjusted return on the dispersion universe.
terms: L re-selected once on rows <= cutoff at fire time, frozen; run once on the virgin rows with the v6 cost ladder + drawdown diagnostic; writes alloc12.SHOT; reruns and other families refuse; do not edit this file after commit
